<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Castell Dinas Brân</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1260</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Dinas Bran</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Castell Dinas Brân is a ruined medieval castle on a steep hill above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llangollen">Llangollen</a> in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. It was built around 1260 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gruffydd-maelor-i">Gruffydd ap Madog</a>, prince of Powys Fadog, on the site of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hillfort. Burnt by its Welsh defenders in 1277 to keep it from the English, it was never seriously reoccupied.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/castell-dinas-bran.jpg" alt="Castell Dinas Brân" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simaron from Lower Saxony, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Above_the_Valley_(52831034885).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name is usually taken as the crow's fortress, since dinas meant a defended enclosure in Middle Welsh and brân is a crow; others derived it from a person named Brân or, as Thomas Pennant and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-lhuyd">Edward Lhuyd</a> held, from a dark stream at the foot of the hill. In English it has been called Crow Castle since at least the 18th century. Around 600 BCE the Ordovices built a large hillfort on the summit, with an earthen rampart and a deep ditch on the gentler southern slopes. A wooden castle of the princes of Powys Fadog, perhaps of Madog ap Gruffydd Maelor, founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valle-crucis-abbey">Valle Crucis Abbey</a>, preceded the stone one and was destroyed by fire.</p><p>Gruffydd II ap Madog, an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a>, built the stone castle as one of several raised after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-montgomery">Treaty of Montgomery</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolforwyn-castle">Dolforwyn Castle</a> may be the work of the same master mason. When Edward I invaded North Wales in 1277, Henry de Lacy, Earl of Lincoln, came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswestry">Oswestry</a> to take it, but its defenders, probably the younger sons Owain and Gruffudd, had already set it alight. After the war of 1282 the castle passed to John de Warenne, Earl of Surrey, who built a new castle at Holt instead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Rectangular stone walls run east to west above a steep northern slope, with a rock-cut ditch 6.1 metres deep and a counterscarp bank on the gentler southern and eastern sides; the ditch also served as a quarry. The two-storey keep stood at the south-eastern corner beside a gatehouse with two towers, once reached by a wooden bridge. On the south side stand the Great Hall, with its enlarged windows, and the D-shaped Welsh Tower, which held the kitchens below and living quarters above. A postern gate in the south-western corner allowed the garrison to sally out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The castle first appears in literature in the 13th-century romance Fouke le Fitz Waryn, where the Norman knight Payn Peveril spends a night in the ruined Chastiel Bran and kills the giant Gogmagog. The 14th-century love story of Myfanwy Fychan of the castle and the poet Hywel ab Einion probably inspired the words of Joseph Parry's song Myfanwy of 1875. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-m-w-turner">J. M. W. Turner</a> and Richard Wilson painted it. A scheduled ancient monument kept by Denbighshire County Council with Cadw, it saw its first archaeological investigation in 2017, followed by an excavation around the gatehouse in 2021.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Castell Dinas Brân” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Madog ap Gruffydd founded the Cistercian abbey of Valle Crucis around 1201, and Gruffydd ap Madog built <b>Castell Dinas Bran</b> around 1260.</blockquote><a href="/state/powys-fadog">Powys Fadog</a></li></ul></section>
